+++ b/docs/user/modbus-tcp.md
@@ -0,0 +1,115 @@
+# Modbus TCP
+
+The charger can run a Modbus TCP server so that an energy management system
+(EMS) can read its status and set the charge power or current. The register
+map is compatible with the smart1 EMS register layout used by Kinetos
+wallboxes, so an EMS that supports those wallboxes can talk to OpenEVSE.
+
+## Security warning
+
+Modbus TCP has **no authentication and no encryption**. Anyone who can reach
+the port can start, stop and limit charging. The HTTP username and password
+do not protect it.
+
+- The server is **off by default**.
+- Only enable it on a trusted local network.
+- Never forward the port to the internet.
+
+## Enable it
+
+There is no settings page yet. Set the options with the `/config` API:
+
+```bash
+curl -X POST http://openevse.local/config \
+  -H 'Content-Type: application/json' \
+  -d '{"modbus_tcp_enabled": true}'
+```
+
+| Option | Default | Meaning |
+|---|---|---|
+| `modbus_tcp_enabled` | `false` | Start the Modbus TCP server |
+| `modbus_tcp_port` | `502` | TCP port |
+
+The server answers unit ID 1 (0 and 255 are accepted too). It supports
+function codes 3 and 4 (read) and 6 and 16 (write). Up to 4 clients can be
+connected at once; a client that sends nothing for 5 minutes is disconnected.
+
+Errors: unsupported function → exception 1, unknown register → exception 2,
+bad count or length → exception 3, other unit ID → exception 11.
+
+## Registers
+
+32-bit values use two registers, **low word first**. Unused addresses inside
+a block read as 0. Addresses outside the blocks return exception 2.
+
+### Status (999–1006, read)
+
+| Register | Meaning |
+|---|---|
+| 999 | Always 0 |
+| 1000 | State: 0 off/sleeping, 2 ready, 7 vehicle connected, 9 charging, 10/11 ventilation required, 12 charge finished, 13 error, 14 GFCI fault |
+| 1001 | Sub state: 0 idle, 1 waiting for RFID, 2 charging, 4 paused, 5 finished, 7 error |
+| 1002 | 1 = available, 0 = disabled or sleeping |
+| 1003 | 1 = free to use, 2/3 = RFID authorisation required |
+| 1004 | Maximum hardware current (A) |
+| 1005 | Maximum configured current (A) |
+| 1006 | Current charge current setpoint (A) |
+
+### Measurements (1099–1111, read)
+
+| Register | Meaning |
+|---|---|
+| 1099 | Always 0 |
+| 1100–1101 | Total power (W, 32 bit) |
+| 1102–1107 | Power L1, L2, L3 (W, 32 bit each). Read 0: there is no per-phase measurement yet |
+| 1108–1109 | Total energy (kWh, 32 bit) |
+| 1110 | Minimum current (A) |
+| 1111 | Controller fault state (OpenEVSE state number) while in error, else 0 |
+
+### Session (1199–1228, read)
+
+| Register | Meaning |
+|---|---|
+| 1220–1221 | Session energy (Wh, 32 bit) |
+| 1222 | Session duration (s, max 65535) |
+| 1224–1225 | Session start time as hhmmss (local time, 32 bit) |
+| 1226–1227 | Session stop time as hhmmss (local time, 32 bit) |
+| 1228 | Session: 0 none, 1 charging, 2 paused, 3 finished |
+
+1299–1327 is the second charge point of the layout and always reads 0.
+
+### Control (read and write)
+
+There are two blocks with the same layout. Use one of them.
+
+| Power block | Current block | Meaning |
+|---|---|---|
+| 1599 | 1999 | Setpoint. Power block: W, converted to amps with the measured voltage and 1 or 3 phases (`is_threephase`). Current block: 0.01 A, 600–8000 (6–80 A) |
+| 1600 | 2000 | 1 = pause while charging |
+| 1601 | 2001 | 1 = enable charging, 2 = disable charging |
+| 1602 | 2002 | Watchdog timeout (s), 0 = off |
+
+- Writes act like any other API client: OpenEVSE uses a claim with API
+  priority, so manual override, RFID, OCPP, limits and safety still win. The
+  claim shows up in `/claims` as client 65552 (`0x10010`).
+- The setpoint is clamped to the charger's minimum and maximum current.
+- Watchdog: if a timeout is set and no new setpoint arrives in time, charging
+  is paused. Write start/stop = 1 again to resume.
+- OpenEVSE extension: when setpoint, pause and start/stop are 0 in **both**
+  blocks, OpenEVSE releases the claim and the charger goes back to its normal
+  behaviour (schedule, solar divert, …).
+- A write to several registers with function 16 is applied as one command.
+
+## Example
+
+Read the status block with [mbpoll](https://github.com/epsilonrt/mbpoll):
+
+```bash
+mbpoll -m tcp -a 1 -r 1000 -c 7 -0 -1 openevse.local
+```
+
+Set 10 A with a 60 s watchdog and enable charging (function 16):
+
+```bash
+mbpoll -m tcp -a 1 -r 1999 -0 -1 openevse.local 1000 0 1 60
+```
